 ## Subtopics
 
 ### Recolorable Textures with Palette Color
-Extends textured Gaussians with a learnable palette color $c_{pal}$ to enable real-time color editing by shifting the view-independent color [S2]. The view-independent color $c_{ind}$ of the $i$-th Gaussian at intersection point $p_i$ is defined as $c_{ind} = c_{pal} + (c_{tex})_i(p_i)$, where $c_{pal}$ is a learnable palette color parameter shared across all Gaussians in a basic scene, and $(c_{tex})_i(p_i)$ is the sampled texture color [S2]. $c_{pal}$ is initialized as the mean color of input multi-view images and is optimized using an RGB reconstruction loss combined with L1 sparsity regularization on $c_{tex}$ [S2].
+Extends Textured-GS with a learnable palette color $\mathbf{c}_{pal}$ to enable real-time color editing by shifting the view-independent color [S2]. The view-independent color $\mathbf{c}_{ind}$ of the $i$-th Gaussian at intersection point $\mathbf{p}_i$ is defined as $\mathbf{c}_{ind} = \mathbf{c}_{pal} + (\mathbf{c}_{tex})_i(\mathbf{p}_i)$, where $\mathbf{c}_{pal}$ is shared across all Gaussians in a basic scene and initialized as the mean color of input multi-view images [S2]. Optimization uses RGB reconstruction loss combined with L1 sparsity regularization on $\mathbf{c}_{tex}$ [S2].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-**Time and Memory Complexity:**
-*   **Memory:** Uses less training memory than baseline methods with comparable Gaussian counts. On the Bicycle Scene (Mip-NeRF 360), it uses 4.53GB memory compared to 5.46GB for Mini-Splatting [S1]. However, it has a slightly larger file size (164MB vs 132MB for Mini-Splatting) due to the 4-channel SH storage [S1].
-*   **Speed:** Rendering speed is comparable to Mini-Splatting. On the Bicycle Scene, it achieves 161 FPS [S1]. Training time is 10m52s on the Bicycle Scene [S1].
-
-**Measured Performance:**
-*   **Mip-NeRF 360:** SSIM 0.828, PSNR 28.54, LPIPS 0.189 with 0.49M Gaussians [S1]. Outperforms standard 3DGS (SSIM 0.815, PSNR 27.69) and Mini-Splatting (SSIM 0.822, PSNR 25.59) [S1].
-*   **Tanks&Temples:** SSIM 0.841, PSNR 23.14, LPIPS 0.183 with 0.20M Gaussians [S1].
-*   **Deep Blending:** SSIM 0.903, PSNR 29.41, LPIPS 0.243 with 0.35M Gaussians [S1].
-
-**Strengths and Limitations:**
-*   **Strengths:** Achieves comparable or superior rendering quality to standard 3DGS and Mini-Splatting while requiring significantly fewer Gaussians [S1]. Effectively addresses sharp feature representation and serrated edges seen in low-count Gaussian methods [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires incremental gradient updates which may complicate implementation compared to batch updates [S1]. The removal of adaptive control modules means the method relies on the quality of the initial Gaussian distribution (e.g., from Mini-Splatting) and cannot dynamically adjust density [S1].
+*   **Performance:** On Mip-NeRF 360, Textured-GS achieves SSIM 0.828, PSNR 28.54, LPIPS 0.189 with 0.49M Gaussians [S1]. On Tanks&Temples, SSIM 0.841, PSNR 23.14, LPIPS 0.183 with 0.20M Gaussians [S1]. On Deep Blending, SSIM 0.903, PSNR 29.41, LPIPS 0.243 with 0.35M Gaussians [S1].
+*   **Resource Usage:** On the Bicycle Scene (Mip-NeRF 360), it achieves 161 FPS, 10m52s train time, 4.53GB memory, and 164MB file size [S1].
+*   **Comparison:** Outperforms standard 3DGS (SSIM 0.815, PSNR 27.69) and Mini-Splatting (SSIM 0.822, PSNR 25.59) on Mip-NeRF 360 [S1]. Requires significantly fewer Gaussians than standard 3DGS for comparable quality [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Enhances sharp feature representation and local texture detail without increasing Gaussian count [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Slightly higher storage due to 4-channel SH [S1]. Rendering speed and training time have room for optimization [S1].
 
 ## Background
-Textured-GS was introduced to address the bottleneck of Gaussian representation in rendering performance, where standard Gaussians exhibit only a single color from a specific viewing angle [S1]. The method adapts the Mini-Splatting architecture to integrate textured Gaussians, validating the approach on pre-optimized 3D Gaussian scenes [S1]. It leverages the differentiable rendering process of 3D Gaussian Splatting [11] but modifies the SH evaluation to use spatial surface vectors [S1].
